{"id": "2cfb58ee-a65a-47ca-a83d-c602d2175598", "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:22:55.279261", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "198e81cc-96fc-4de3-a10f-f53c2d8027e1", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "## (A) Research question & operationalized hypotheses\n\n**Research question.** *Will BAdam achieve better performance on zero-shot math benchmarks compared to LoRA when finetuning Llama 3-70B on MathInstruct?*\n\n**Definition of \"better\".** The headline question is answered under **three pre-registered framings**, so the conclusion cannot be cherry-picked from a single test:\n\n| Framing | Definition on the paired-difference Δ = mean(BAdam) − mean(LoRA) across 5 seeds | Headline verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| (i) **Superiority** | Δ > 0 with one-sided paired t-test (or Wilcoxon if normality violated) and Holm-Bonferroni-corrected p < 0.05 on **≥ 1 primary benchmark** (GSM8K or MATH). | BAdam is **better** if (i) holds. |\n| (ii) **Non-inferiority** | \\|Δ\\| ≤ **1.0 percentage point** with two-sided corrected p > 0.05 on **both primary benchmarks**. Margins are pre-registered; 1pp is justified by the literature's 5-seed paired-std at LoRA r≈32 reported on GSM8K/MATH (typical σ_paired ≈ 0.4–0.7pp). | BAdam is **at least as good** if (i) fails but (ii) holds. |\n| (iii) **Pareto (accuracy vs peak memory)** | Report (accuracy, peak GPU memory) per cell. BAdam is **Pareto-better** than LoRA on a benchmark if its accuracy lower 95 % CI bound ≥ LoRA's *and* its peak memory is strictly lower; otherwise we report its Pareto position. | Memory-efficient BAdam claim honored. |\n\nThe BAdam paper's headline thesis makes framing (i) the most aggressive hypothesis on the harder MATH benchmark and framing (iii) the headline claim on memory. Framings (i)/(ii) are reported on primary benchmarks; framing (iii) is reported on all four.\n\n**Operationalized hypotheses per benchmark** (formally pre-registered as the alternative for the paired test):\n\n* H_a (GSM8K, BAdam-Full vs LoRA-r32): Δ > 0 with corrected p < 0.05.\n* H_a (MATH, BAdam-Full vs LoRA-r32): Δ > 0 with corrected p < 0.05.\n* H_a (GSM8K, BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 vs LoRA-r32 [fairness mirror]): Δ > 0 with corrected p < 0.05 (per (i)); non-inferiority per (ii) is the secondary claim.\n* H_a (SVAMP; AQuA-RAT): same three framings, reported but framed as secondary (no primary claim of superiority is made).\n\n**Primary cells.** Two cells carry the headline claims:\n\n* **Cell P (primary):** LoRA(r=32, α=64, all-linear) vs **BAdam-Full** (the BAdam paper's headline memory-efficient *full*-parameter SFT). This is the \"spirit of the question\" — same compute envelope, different parameterization.\n* **Cell F (fairness-mirror, secondary cell reported alongside P):** LoRA(r=32, α=64, all-linear) vs **BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=32)** — trainable scalar parameter count matched within 1× (414.2M vs 414.2M); differs from LoRA only in (a) the parameter update is restricted per active block and (b) optimizer state cycling. This isolates \"is block-wise update dynamics the lever?\" from \"does full-parameter training matter?\".\n\nBoth cells are reported for all three framings.\n\n---\n\n## (B) Materials\n\n### B.1 Model\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Subject model | `meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-70B` (the **base / pretrained / non-Instruct** checkpoint) |\n| Precision | **bf16** for all frozen weights and forward/backward; **fp32 adapters** for LoRA / BAdam-Block-LoRA; **fp32 master + bf16 param** for full-param blocks in BAdam-Full |\n| Training regime | Standard causal-LM next-token prediction with teacher-forcing on the MathInstruct response (prompt tokens masked in loss) |\n| Tokenizer | the model's own BPE (tiktoken-backed in HF `transformers`); pad token = the dedicated `<|reserved_special_token_…|>` if none set; **no chat-template tokens** are added at train or eval because the base model is not chat-tuned |\n| License / gating access | Llama-3 Community License; HF gated model — assumed **access granted** for design-only purposes; verification required before execution |\n\n### B.2 Dataset\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Training data | **TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct** (Yue et al., 2023) — combined instruction-response set from 13 math-reasoning sources, ~262K total |\n| HF path | `TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct` |\n| License | Per source (Apache-2.0 / CC-BY-SA / research-only mix). Documented per HF card; if any source is research-only it is preserved but flagged in results. |\n| Hash pinning | Pin `revision` + `sha` of the dataset before training |\n| Pre-processing | (a) deduplicate near-duplicates (`minhash` 32-bands, Jaccard ≥ 0.85 collapses); (b) drop items whose response is empty or non-mathematical per a regex preprocess; (c) standardize to `{instruction, output}` two-field schema |\n| **Primary condition** | **Stratified 80K subsample** preserving the 13-source proportions; trained for **2 epochs** (≈ 160K example passes) |\n| **Dev split** | Held-out 2K examples from MathInstruct train, sampled *before* any contamination check on benchmarks; used for early-stop and dev-loss tracking only (no test items) |\n| **Scale-robustness condition** | Full 262K, 1 epoch; reported as a secondary arm to confirm (i) does not reverse at scale |\n\n### B.3 Hardware assumption (resolves U7)\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Assumed envelope | **2–4× H100 or H200 80 GB** with NVLink/NVSwitch |\n| Frameworks | DeepSpeed **ZeRO-3** + **gradient checkpointing** + **bf16 mixed precision** |\n| BAdam-Full cell | Requires **upper bound** (3–4× H100) with **8-bit AdamW** (`bitsandbytes.optim.AdamW8bit`) for the active block's optimizer state |\n| Fallback | Reduce seeds to **3** if budget is constrained; document the seed-number change before pre-registration is broken |\n\n### B.4 Unknown-but-used assets (no execution implied)\n\nWe **only describe behaviorally**; we do not assume their internal correctness:\n* The `peft` LoRA implementation (Hu et al., 2021 re-impl) ✓ in `peft.LoraConfig`.\n* The BAdam implementation, referenced as Luo et al. 2024 (paper): block-wise AdamW with CPU-offload/rotation of inactive-block optimizer state. We require that the implementation expose: (i) block count `B`, (ii) `warmup_steps_before_rotation`, (iii) the rotation schedule, (iv) per-block activation bf16. Cross-checked once via a 1B smoke test before the 70B run; if behavior deviates from paper, design is held and a new pre-registration is written.\n\n---\n\n## (C) Methods\n\n### C.1 Baseline arm — LoRA (PEFT)\n\n```\nLoRA config         : r ∈ {16, 32, 64} ; α = 2r ; dropout = 0.05\nTrainable targets   : all linear projections\n                       {q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj,\n                        gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj}\n                       in every Transformer block (80 layers)\nFrozen              : embed_tokens, LM head, RMSNorm, all base linear weights\nOptimizer           : AdamW(β=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0) — applies only to adapters\nLR schedule         : linear warmup 3% of total → cosine to 0\nPeak LR search      : {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}; 1 seed per cell; chosen = lowest dev loss;\n                       threshold:   |Δdev_loss| < 2% → pick smaller LR;\n                                   otherwise pick the LR with min dev loss.\nFrozen headline LR : chosen LR frozen for the 5-seed headline run.\nHeadline            : r=32, α=64 (414.2 M trainable scalar params — see computation below)\nSensitivities       : r=16 (207.1 M), r=64 (828.4 M)\n```\n\n**Trainable-parameter count derivation (used for budget matching):** with Llama-3-70B hidden=8192, kv_heads=8 (head_dim=128), intermediate=28672, 80 layers, the all-linear target module total is ≈ 68.45 B trainable target params (Q+O = 8192² × 2 per layer = 134.2 M; K+V = 8192 × 1024 × 2 per layer = 16.8 M; MLP = 3 × 8192 × 28672 per layer ≈ 704.6 M; total per layer ≈ 855.6 M; ×80 ≈ 68.45 B). LoRA adds `r × (in+out)` per module per layer; summing over all seven modules per layer × 80 layers yields **414.2 M at r=32**, 207.1 M at r=16, 828.4 M at r=64.\n\n### C.2 Primary arm — BAdam-Full (full-parameter block-wise Adam)\n\n```\nBlock partition    : 10 blocks; 8 contiguous Transformer layers per block\nRotation schedule  : cyclic, block index advances by 1 every optimizer step\nWarmup             : step-wise warm-up of LR for 3% of total optimizer steps;\n                       then 1 epoch (sequential) of \"warm-up the first block to convergence\" before rotation begins (matches paper's 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup)\nOptimizer          : AdamW(β=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0); 8-bit AdamW (bitsandbytes)\n                     for the *active* block's optimizer state only;\n                     inactive-block optimizer states are CPU-offloaded\nParam representation: per-parameter master copy in fp32 + bf16 weight buffer\nTrainable          : all 70B parameters (excluding embed_tokens and LM head which are frozen throughout)\nLR schedule        : linear warmup 3% of total → cosine to 0\nPeak LR search     : {1e-5, 2e-5}    (tightened vs LoRA: per paper intuition\n                                       that full-parameter SFT needs smaller LR)\nHeadline           : LR chosen by dev-loss; frozen for 5-seed headline run\n```\n\n### C.3 Fairness-mirror arm — BAdam-Block-LoRA\n\n```\nBaseline block part.: 10 contiguous layer blocks, 8 layers/block\nRotation + warmup   : identical to BAdam-Full\nTrainable targets   : the LoRA adapters inside the active block only; at every\n                       step, only that block's adapters receive gradients; the\n                       inactive blocks' adapters are forward-passed and\n                       skipped from optimizer state\nOptimizer           : AdamW(β=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0); fp32 (small)\nLoRA config         : identical r, α, dropout to the *paired* LoRA cell\nParam-budget match  : LoRA(r=32) all-linear: 414.2 M trainable;\n                       BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=32): 414.2 M trainable (exact match);\n                       BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=16, r=64) as sensitivities\n                       (≤ 2× budget rule ⇒ r=32 is the headlined choice)\n```\n\n### C.4 Alignment variables (locked)\n\n| Aligned variable | How it is matched | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total tokens seen | identical (same packed-sequence bucketing: pack to 4096 tokens, first-fit; 80K examples ≈ 10K packed sequences; 2 epochs ≈ 20K seqs) | drives compute |\n| Optimizer steps | identical | batch size fixed at 2 micro × 4 grad-accum = 8 effective batch per \"packed sequence\" = effective token batch = 32 768 |\n| Effective batch | identical | 32 768 tokens/step; 80K condition → ~625 steps/epoch × 2 epochs ≈ 1 250 steps |\n| Wall-clock-per-token | identical (same ZeRO-3, same activation checkpointing, same bf16 precision); BAdam uses 8-bit Adam where LoRA uses fp32 | reported measured wall-clock per cell as Pareto covariate |\n| Data order | same per-seed seed-init RNG; same shuffled indices fed to both paired cells | ensures *paired* random effects |\n| Tokenization | identical tokenizer call, identical prompt template | no leakage from prompt variance |\n| Init of trainable params | stable seed init, frozen base seed | matched |\n| Eval template / extractor / decoding | identical across cells | matched |\n| Peak GPU memory | **not** matched — measured as the Pareto covariate (this is exactly what (iii) reports) |  |\n\n**What is not matched (intentionally).**\n\n* Peak LR is allowed to differ *between methods* (each method's LR is tuned to its own dev loss within {1e-5, 2e-5, [5e-5 only for LoRA]}); once chosen, LR is frozen across methods for headline comparisons.\n* Trainable parameter count is *intentionally* imbalanced between Cell P's two arms (414M vs 70B) because that is the question; it is *intentionally* balanced within Cell F (414M vs 414M).\n\n### C.5 Training schedule (locked)\n\n```\nTraining objective  : causal LM NLL on response tokens (prompt masked via labels=-100)\nSequence handling   : pack to 4096-token sequences; positional + causal masks built\n                       correctly with attention island isolation across documents (flash-attn var-len)\nDropout             : adapter dropout 0.05 (LoRA, BAdam-Block-LoRA);\n                       0 (full-param blocks have no per-layer dropout in BAdam-Full —\n                          paper convention)\nGradient clipping   : global L2-norm 1.0\nMixed precision     : bf16 forward + bf16 backward; fp32 master weights where used;\n                       8-bit AdamW for active block in BAdam-Full\nCheckpointing       : activation checkpointing on every Transformer block\nCheckpoint cadence  : every 250 steps; keep best-3 by dev loss; final = best-by-dev\nEarly-stop          : patience 3 evals (each eval = 1/4 epoch step on dev slice)\nTotal tokens        : 80K condition × ~410 avg tokens × 2 epochs ≈ 65.6 M tokens\n                       (exact token count recorded at run time and reused for full-262K scale arm)\n```\n\n---\n\n## (D) Evaluation\n\n### D.1 Benchmark panel\n\n| Tier | Benchmark | Size | Task | Primary metric |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Primary** | GSM8K (Cobbe et al., 2021) | 1 319 test | grade-school word-problems, free-form | exact-match @ greedy |\n| **Primary** | MATH (Hendrycks et al., 2021) | 5 000 test | competition math, free-form with `\\boxed{}` | exact-match via MATH-grader equivalence (`minervamath` or `math_verify`) |\n| Secondary | SVAMP (Patel et al., 2021) | 300 test | arithmetic word-problem variations | exact-match @ greedy |\n| Secondary | AQuA-RAT (Ling et al., 2017) | 254 test | algebra, multiple-choice A–E | option-accuracy (single letter) |\n\nAQuA-RAT is **dropped from primary analyses if its contamination rate exceeds 2 %** (resolves U6). It remains in secondary. SVAMP is similarly audited.\n\n### D.2 Prompting\n\n* **Single canonical template (frozen).** For all benchmarks, all seeds, all cells:\n\n  ```\n  Q: <question>\n  A:\n  ```\n\n* The base model is **not chat-tuned**, so no chat-template tokens are inserted. Sensitivity at eval (a separate secondary table) repeats evaluation under the MathInstruct SFT format:\n\n  ```\n  <|begin_of_text|>Instruction: <question>\n  Response:\n  ```\n\n* **Zero-shot.** No in-context examples. Few-shot = 0 (this is required by the research question).\n\n### D.3 Decoding\n\n* Greedy decoding, `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0.0`.\n* `max_new_tokens = 512` for MATH; `256` for GSM8K, SVAMP, AQuA.\n* Stop on EOS or `\\n\\nQ:` (whichever comes first).\n* No length penalty; no repetition penalty.\n\n### D.4 Answer extraction (identical across cells)\n\nA two-stage deterministic extractor:\n\n1. **`\\boxed{…}` regex** → first match used (MATH primary path). Multi-box collapse by chosen-order.\n2. **`The answer is X`** regex → fallback (covers a subset of GSM8K / SVAMP that includes verbal answer phrases).\n3. **Last-number-in-string** fallback (GSM8K / SVAMP).\n4. **Letter regex `\\(?[A-E]\\)?`** for AQuA: pick the last matched option.\n\nThe same extractor is run on both LoRA and BAdam outputs; outputs are normalized via Unicode normalization and `\\frac{a}{b}` → `a/b` standardization before exact-match scoring (MATH only).\n\n### D.5 Contamination gate (resolves U6)\n\n1. **Construction.** For each candidate `{test item, prompt + expected answer}` and each MathInstruct *train* example, compute:\n    * 25-gram character overlap (with whitespace tokens) on the prompt string;\n    * full-string match `prompt == train.string`;\n    * answer-token Jaccard ≥ 0.9 on answer-bearing sub-strings.\n2. **Drop rule.** Drop any test item **before scoring** if any one of (a) ≥25-gram prompt overlap, (b) full-string match on prompt, (c) answer Jaccard ≥ 0.9.\n3. **Reporting.** Per benchmark: contamination rate = (dropped) / (original test size) reported with the headline numbers.\n4. **Hard gate.** AQuA-RAT → if contamination rate > 2 %, AQuA is moved from secondary to *excluded* (reported only as a footnote). Same rule for SVAMP.\n5. **GSM8K / MATH test sets** are held-out by construction in their original releases; expected contamination ≈ 0.\n\n### D.6 What gets logged\n\nPer run, per benchmark: per-item prompt, response, extracted answer, gold answer, exact-match verdict, tokens-decoded, seconds. Saved as a CSV/JSONL log; aggregated into per-seed accuracy and per-cell accuracy tables.\n\n---\n\n## (E) Statistical analysis plan\n\n### E.1 Sample size and pairing\n\n* `n_seeds = 5` per cell (default; 3 if budget-constrained, with pre-registration update).\n* Each seed `s` produces paired observations `(LoRA_s, BAdam_s)` trained on the identical seed-init token stream. Differences are computed cell-wise.\n\n### E.2 Test statistic\n\nFor each benchmark B and Cell P or F:\n\n1. Compute Δ_s(B) = accuracy_s(BAdam, B) − accuracy_s(LoRA, B) for s = 1..5.\n2. **Normality check** on Δ_s(B) via Shapiro–Wilk. If p ≥ 0.05 → use **paired t-test** (two-sided for non-inferiority, one-sided for superiority). If p < 0.05 → fall back to **Wilcoxon signed-rank** (two-sided for non-inferiority, one-sided for superiority).\n3. **Effect size:** **Cohen's d_z** = mean(Δ_s(B)) / SD(Δ_s(B)).\n4. **Confidence interval:** **BCa bootstrap** on Δ_s(B) with B = 10 000 resamples, 95 % CI.\n5. **Multiple-comparisons correction:** within each cell, the four benchmarks are corrected by **Holm–Bonferroni** at α = 0.05.\n\n### E.3 Acceptance verdicts (all three framings)\n\n* **(i) Superiority.** BAdam wins on a benchmark if Δ̂ > 0 **and** corrected p < 0.05 (one-sided). Headline answer (i) = at least one primary benchmark wins.\n* **(ii) Non-inferiority** (margin m = 1 pp = 0.01). BAdam is non-inferior on B if |Δ̂ − 0| ≤ m and the **two-sided** corrected test p > 0.05 (i.e., we cannot reject the null that |Δ| > m). Headline (ii) = both primary benchmarks meet it.\n* **(iii) Pareto.** Plot accuracy (95 % CI) vs peak GPU memory per cell, per primary benchmark. BAdam is Pareto-better on B iff its lower-CI bound ≥ LoRA's accuracy **and** its peak memory is strictly lower; otherwise report Pareto position.\n\n### E.4 Pre-registration\n\nBefore any training: write the pre-registration document listing\n* all locked hyperparameters (LR, schedule, batch, BCa iterations, Holm family size),\n* the exact extractor / decoder / prompt template,\n* the contamination rule and per-benchmark drop counts,\n* the seed list,\n* the three pre-registered framings and their decision thresholds.\n\nCommit the pre-registration (hash-pinned) before unlocking training. Any later change re-opens pre-registration and is reported with explicit `deviation` tags.\n\n---\n\n## (F) Design matrix table\n\n| ID | Cell | Method | r | Other LoRA params | BAdam block config | Training data | Epochs | n_seeds | LR (peak) | Eval panel |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P-LoRA-hd | Cell P | LoRA all-linear (headline) | **32** | α=64, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | tuned ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}, then frozen | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\n| P-BAdam-Full | Cell P | BAdam-Full | n/a | n/a | 10 blocks, cyclic, 8 layers/block, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | tuned ∈ {1e-5, 2e-5}, then frozen | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\n| F-LoRA-hd | Cell F | LoRA all-linear (headline) | **32** | α=64, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | shared LR (same cell P-LoRA-hd lock) | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\n| F-BAdam-Block-LoRA-hd | Cell F | BAdam-Block-LoRA | **32** | α=64, dropout=0.05 | 10 blocks, cyclic, 8 layers/block, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | shared LR | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\n| Sens-LoRA-r16 | sensitivity | LoRA all-linear | 16 | α=32, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\n| Sens-LoRA-r64 | sensitivity | LoRA all-linear | 64 | α=128, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\n| Sens-BBLoRA-r16 | sensitivity | BAdam-Block-LoRA | 16 | α=32, dropout=0.05 | same as F-block | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\n| Sens-BBLoRA-r64 | sensitivity | BAdam-Block-LoRA | 64 | α=128, dropout=0.05 | same as F-block | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\n| LR-sweep * | hyper only | LoRA-r32 + BAdam-Full + BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 | 32 | α=64 | n/a / 10 blocks | 80K subsample | 1 LR-sweep only | 1 each | full LR set above | dev only |\n| Scale-LoRA | robustness | LoRA-r32 | 32 | α=64 | n/a | full 262K | 1 | 3 | frozen from headline | GSM8K, MATH |\n| Scale-BAdam-Full | robustness | BAdam-Full | n/a | n/a | same | full 262K | 1 | 3 | frozen from headline | GSM8K, MATH |\n\nNotes:\n\\* Each LR-sweep cell only inspects dev-loss and does not enter headline inference. It costs one seed per {method × LR} combination but ties to the headline seeds for the chosen LR combination.\n\nCell counts:\n* Headline cells: 4 (P-LoRA-hd, P-BAdam-Full, F-LoRA-hd, F-BAdam-Block-LoRA-hd) × 5 seeds × 4 benchmarks = **80 (method, seed, benchmark) headline observations**.\n* Sensitivity cells: 4 × 3 seeds × 2 benchmarks = **24 sensitivity observations**.\n* Robustness cells: 2 × 3 seeds × 2 benchmarks = **12 robustness observations**.\n* Total headline + secondary: **116 (method, seed, benchmark) cells**, none of them executed in this design-only task.\n\n---\n\n## (G) Feasibility & cost (envelope estimates; design-only)\n\nThe numbers below are upfront envelope estimates to inform whether the design is realistic; they are not outcomes of the experiment.\n\n### G.1 Memory estimates per cell (peak GPU memory, single training step, seq=4096, micro-batch=2)\n\n| Item | LoRA-r32 (3-4× H100) | BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 (3-4× H100) | BAdam-Full (3-4× H100 80GB + 8-bit Adam) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Frozen base weights (bf16) | 70B × 2B = 140 GB shard via ZeRO-3; per-GPU: 35–47 GB | same | same |\n| Active block fp32 master weights (1 block, fp32) | n/a | 8 layers × 855.6M × 4B ≈ 27.4 GB on one device | same on one device |\n| Active block bf16 weight buffer (1 block) | n/a | 8 × 855.6M × 2B ≈ 13.7 GB (one device) | same (one device) |\n| Adapters (LoRA / BAdam-Block-LoRA, fp32) | 414.2M × 4B ≈ 1.66 GB | 1.66 GB (one device) | n/a |\n| Active-block optimizer state (8-bit) | n/a | n/a (fp32 AdamW since small) | 8 × 855.6M × 2 × 1B ≈ 13.7 GB on one device |\n| Optimizer state (fp32 m+v) for adapters | 414.2M × 8B ≈ 3.31 GB | 3.31 GB on one device | n/a |\n| Active-block gradients (bf16, ZeRO-3 shard) | n/a | small (one block's adapter grads only) | 8 × 855.6M × 2B × (1/4 shard) ≈ 6.85 GB per device |\n| Adapter gradients (fp32) | 1.66 GB | 1.66 GB | n/a |\n| Activations (with grad ckpt, seq=4096, mb=2) | 5–10 GB per GPU | 5–10 GB | 10–20 GB per GPU |\n| Misc / framework overhead | 2 GB | 2 GB | 4 GB |\n| **Peak per-GPU estimate** | **~50–60 GB** (2× H100 feasible; 3–4× H100 comfortable) | **~50–60 GB** (2× H100 feasible; 3–4× H100 comfortable) | **~70–95 GB** at the device hosting the active block; **3–4× H100 80GB required** |\n\n### G.2 Wall-clock estimates (design-only envelope)\n\nAssumptions: 4×H100 80GB, bf16, ZeRO-3, grad ckpt, internal-throughput ≈ 1.5× A100 (rule of thumb).\n\n| Step | LoRA-r32 / BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 per step | BAdam-Full per step |\n|---|---|---|\n| fwd+bwd+optimizer | ~3–5 s/step (with ZeRO-3 all-gather) | ~6–10 s/step (full-param fwd+bwd dominates) |\n| Total steps (headline) | ≈ 1 250 step | ≈ 1 250 step |\n| **One seed train (headline)** | ~1.5–2 hours | ~3–4 hours |\n| **Five-seed train (headline)** | ~7.5–10 hours | ~15–20 hours |\n| Eval (4 benchmarks × 5 seeds, full test sets) | ~1.5–3 hours | ~1.5–3 hours |\n| Per-cell total (headline, 5 seeds + eval) | ~9–13 hours | ~17–23 hours |\n\nAggregate envelope (design-only):\n* Cell P headline (LoRA-r32): **~10 H100-hours**.\n* Cell P headline (BAdam-Full): **~20 H100-hours**.\n* Cell F headline (BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32): **~10 H100-hours**.\n* All sensibility + LR sweep + 80K condition: **~+15 H100-hours**.\n* Full-262K robustness × 2 methods × 3 seeds: **~+30 H100-hours**.\n* **Total envelope:** **~80–90 H100-hours**, fitting in ~24 h on a 4×H100 node.\n\n### G.3 Risk mitigations (design-only)\n\n1. **Reduce to 3 seeds if 5-seed envelope is breached.** Document the deviation explicitly in the pre-registration deviation log; verify power by recomputing BCa CI half-width at the realized σ_paired.\n2. **Smoke-test on 1B before 70B.** Run 1 hour on a 1B-class base (Llama-3.2-1B or LLaMA-2-7B) to validate the BAdam implementation behaves as the paper describes (block cycle, 1-epoch pre-rotation, off-loader turnover). Pre-registration break is not required for this 1B smoke test (its outcomes are not in the headline analysis).\n3. **Checkpoint frequently.** Every 250 steps, keep best-3 by dev loss.\n4. **Implement a step-time and memory logger** to validate Pareto covariate (framing iii) is comparable across cells.\n\n---\n\n## (H) Threats to validity\n\n* **Contamination.** GSM8K/MATH test sets are held out, but MathInstruct was *built from various training sets*; the audit per §D.5 with a 2 % hard gate is the primary mitigation. AQuA and SVAMP are uplift-prone; their possible exclusion is pre-registered.\n* **Prompt sensitivity.** A single canonical template (no chat tokens) is used to remove prompt variance. A sensitivity secondary table repeats evaluation under the MathInstruct SFT format; if results disagree, the template used to train (and the base model's non-instruction-tuned nature) is the most plausible cause and is reported as such.\n* **Single-model, single-tokenizer.** Llama-3-70B base is one model; generalization to, e.g., Mistral, Qwen, or Llama-3.1-Instruct is not tested in this design. We do not claim cross-model generalization.\n* **MathInstruct source overlap with SVAMP / AQuA.** Even after the contamination drop rule, residual topical / structural overlap can favor methods that better memorize the train distribution. Mitigation: we report SVAMP/AQuA as secondary only, and we report SVAMP/AQuA exclusion rate on the headline table.\n* **Seed variance at 70B PEFT scale.** With ~10 8 -bit trainable parameters (LoRA-r16) to ~10 9 trainable parameters (LoRA-r32), gradient noise on a 41M-token training budget is the dominant source of variance. 5-seed paired design is the primary mitigation; fallback at 3 seeds is documented.\n* **Method-implementation deviation, not method itself.** BAdam-Full's per-step behavior is sensitive to block-count, rotation policy, and offload scheduling. We adopt the paper's settings (U3) but acknowledge the implementation we use is **not the paper's reference implementation quirk-free**. Smoke test on a 1B model mitigates the most likely failure modes.\n* **LR-asymmetry.** Cell P (BAdam-Full) and Cell P (LoRA) use method-specific peak LRs tuned on dev. This is the most defensible fairness convention in PEFT literature, but is an explicit axis on which cells differ; we report the chosen LRs in the headline.\n* **Pareto measurement.** Peak memory is what is reported; throughput (tokens/sec) is reported separately. The two Pareto axes answer slightly different sub-claims of the BAdam paper, and we report them as such; we do **not** recompute accuracy under any \"matched memory\" capping.\n* **Skill vs. memorization.** MATH's free-form answer extraction honors the boxed-expression convention, but a model that emits verbose prose can fail extraction the same way; the extractor is identical across cells, so the differential effect cancels but absolute numbers may be lower than leader-board numbers from differently-prompted systems.\n\n---\n\n## (I) Reporting plan\n\n**Headline table A.1 — Accuracies per benchmark (mean ± std across seeds; primary condition).**\n\n| Method | GSM8K | MATH | SVAMP | AQuA-RAT (or excluded) | Peak GPU mem |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LoRA-r32 (headline) | mean ± std | … | … | … | X GB |\n| BAdam-Full | … | … | … | … | Y GB |\n| (Δ, 95% BCa CI, p_holm) | per col | per col | per col | per col | n/a |\n\n**Headline table A.2 — Paired-difference verdicts.**\n\n| Benchmark | Δ̂ (BAdam − LoRA) | BCa 95 % CI | Cohen's d_z | corrected p | Verdict (i) sup | Verdict (ii) NI | Verdict (iii) Pareto |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n\nCell P and Cell F each have one row per benchmark.\n\n**Figure plan.**\n* **Fig. 1** — per-benchmark paired-difference forest plot (Cell P, primary condition) with 95 % BCa CIs and the 1pp non-inferiority band.\n* **Fig. 2** — Pareto plot: accuracy on MATH vs peak GPU memory, one marker per (cell, seed), color-coded by method.\n* **Fig. 3** — training curves (dev loss and benchmark accuracy on a held-out 500-example MATH slice) over the 1 250-step run, averaged across seeds; shaded = 95 % BCa band.\n\n**Sensitivity table A.3.**\n* Sensitivity (`r = 16`, `r = 32`, `r = 64`) of LoRA and BAdam-Block-LoRA on the two primary benchmarks; same statistics.\n\n**Robustness table A.4.**\n* Full-262K condition, LoRA-r32 vs BAdam-Full on GSM8K and MATH (3 seeds); only used to cross-check (i)/(ii) does not flip sign at scale.\n\n**Side table A.5** — per-benchmark contamination rate and excluded item count.\n\n**Verdict mapping to the research question** is reported as a single sentence per framing:\n\n* (i) \"BAdam **does better than** LoRA on the math benchmark {GSM8K | MATH | neither}\" iff the one-sided corrected test on ≥ 1 primary benchmark passes.\n* (ii) \"BAdam is **at least as good as** LoRA on the math panel\" iff both primary benchmarks pass the 1pp NI check.\n* (iii) \"BAdam is **a Pareto-improved fit** for this fine-tune\" iff accuracy is non-inferior AND peak memory is strictly lower on at least one primary benchmark.\n\n---\n\n## (J) References\n\n1. Cobbe, K., Kosaraju, V., Bavarian, M., Chen, M., Jun, H., Kaiser, L., Plappert, M., Tworek, J., Hilton, J., Nakano, R., Hesse, C., & Schulman, J. (2021). *Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems.* arXiv:2110.14168.\n2. Hendrycks, D., Burns, C., Kadavath, S., Arora, A., Basart, S., Tang, E., Song, D., & Steinhardt, J. (2021). *Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset.* NeurIPS Datasets and Benchmarks.\n3. Hu, E. J., Shen, Y., Wallis, P., Allen-Zhu, Z., Li, Y., Wang, S., Wang, L., & Chen, W. (2021). *LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.* ICLR.\n4. Ling, W., Yogatama, D., Dyer, C., & Blunsom, P. (2017). *Program Induction by Rationale Generation: Learning to Solve and Explain Algebraic Word Problems.* ACL.\n5. Luo, Q., Yu, X., & others. (2024). *BAdam: A Memory-Efficient Full Parameter Optimization of Large Language Models.* arXiv.\n6. Meta AI. (2024). *The Llama 3 Herd of Models.* (referenced for tokenizer, config, model card).\n7. Patel, A., Bhattamishra, S., & Goyal, N. (2021). *Are NLP Models really able to Solve Simple Math Word Problems?* NAACL.\n8. Yue, X., Qu, X., Zhang, G., Fu, Y., Huang, S., Liu, J., Chen, W., Zeng, J., Wang, L., WANG, Y., Tian, B., Cao, M., Zhang, D., Cao, Y., Yan, J., Liu, T., Ji, S., Chen, Y., & Liu, P. (2023). *MAmmoTH: Building Math Reasoning Models with Hybrid Instruction Data.* (paper releasing MathInstruct as the associated dataset.) arXiv:2309.05653.\n9. Holm, S. (1979). *A simple sequentially rejective multiple test procedure.* Scandinavian Journal of Statistics.\n10. Efron, B. (1987). *Better bootstrap confidence intervals.* JASA. (BCa bootstrap reference.)\n11. `peft` library documentation — HuggingFace, LoRA implementation.\n12. `bitsandbytes` 8-bit optimizer documentation — for AdamW8bit used in BAdam-Full's active block.\n13. DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 documentation — Microsoft.\n14. *MathInstruct* dataset card — `TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct` on HuggingFace.\n\n---\n\n## Closing note (researcher-facing)\n\nThis document is design-only. **No experiment was executed and no outcome is reported or implied.** Estimated wall-clock, memory, and parameter counts in (G) are upfront feasibility envelopes to inform whether the design fits the hardware assumption (B.3 / U7); they will be re-validated by the smoke test (G.3 item 2) before any headline run. The pre-registration content (E.4) is the only authoritative document for headline inference; the table in (F) lists every (method, config, seed, benchmark) cell that would exist if the experiment were run.\n\nAll resolved open questions are written as **fixed design choices** above; what remains assumption-shaped is enumerated in the `assumptions[]` of this handoff for completeness — these are not open questions for the design but are conditions on its scope.\n", "evidence": "Environment evidence: /workspace contains only /workspace/task.json; no track.md file on disk (the documents.track.md field is metadata stating \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"); bubblewrap runtime with disabled network, GPU disabled; Python 3.12.3 with 24 cores, ~125 GiB RAM, ~775 GiB free disk; no ML libraries installed (torch/transformers/peft/trl/vllm absent); HF auth tokens absent; git/pip/curl/wget available. The research question and the design-only instruction are confirmed in /workspace/task.json; no executed experiment was performed. Budget math (414.2 M LoRA-r32 trainable params, 68.45 B target-module total) was computed live in this session from Llama-3-70B's published config (hidden=8192, kv_heads=8, intermediate=28672, layers=80, all-linear targets) to make Cell F's exact trainable-params match internally consistent in (C.D design plan).", "assumptions": ["X1. With Llama-3-70B base (hidden=8192, kv_heads=8, intermediate=28672, layers=80, all-linear LoRA targets), LoRA-r32 yields 414.2 M trainable scalar params and the all-linear target module total is ~68.45 B; used only for budget matching in (C.3) and memory bookkeeping in (G.1).", "X2. HF gated access to Llama-3-70B base is assumed granted for execution purposes; not used in this design-only deliverable.", "X3. The exact per-step wall-clock-per-token in (G.2) is an upfront envelope estimate based on rule-of-thumb throughput for 4 x H100 80GB NVLink at 70B bf16 + ZeRO-3; not a measured outcome.", "X4. Reported typical 5-seed paired std for LoRA-r32 ~ 0.4-0.7pp on GSM8K/MATH comes from analogous PEFT-vs-LoRA comparisons in the BAdam paper and Hu et al. follow-ups; the 1pp non-inferiority margin in (A)/(E.3) is grounded in this range but is itself a pre-registered design choice.", "X5. The implementation of BAdam available to us matches Luo et al. 2024's paper-described behavior (block-wise AdamW with cyclic rotation, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup, CPU offload for inactive-block optimizer state); any deviation is caught by the 1B smoke test (G.3 item 2) before 70B execution."], "uncertainties": ["R1 (informational, low). Llama-3-70B HF gated access verification is required before execution; design-only deliverable does not depend on this.", "R2 (informational, low). Exact revision/sha pin of TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct is fixed at pre-registration time, not at design time.", "R3 (informational, low). Per-step wall-clock and per-device peak memory to be measured at smoke-test time, replacing the envelope in (G.2) and (G.1).", "R4 (informational, low). Benchmarks' \"official answer extraction\" quirks: -MATH grader equivalence (minervamath or math_verify) is locked at pre-registration. If behaviour differs across the two at MATH extraction time, the design specifies minervamath as the default since it is the version the MAmmoTH paper uses for the MathInstruct panel evaluation."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bbae03da-dfd3-4c50-952c-aafa1118315b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bbae03da-dfd3-4c50-952c-aafa1118315b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit final design document (stage=result)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"## (A) Research question & operationalized hypotheses\\n\\n**Research question.** *Will BAdam achieve better performance on zero-shot math benchmarks compared to LoRA when finetuning Llama 3-70B on MathInstruct?*\\n\\n**Definition of \\\"better\\\".** The headline question is answered under **three pre-registered framings**, so the conclusion cannot be cherry-picked from a single test:\\n\\n| Framing | Definition on the paired-difference \\u0394 = mean(BAdam) \\u2212 mean(LoRA) across 5 seeds | Headline verdict |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| (i) **Superiority** | \\u0394 > 0 with one-sided paired t-test (or Wilcoxon if normality violated) and Holm-Bonferroni-corrected p < 0.05 on **\\u2265 1 primary benchmark** (GSM8K or MATH). | BAdam is **better** if (i) holds. |\\n| (ii) **Non-inferiority** | \\\\|\\u0394\\\\| \\u2264 **1.0 percentage point** with two-sided corrected p > 0.05 on **both primary benchmarks**. Margins are pre-registered; 1pp is justified by the literature's 5-seed paired-std at LoRA r\\u224832 reported on GSM8K/MATH (typical \\u03c3_paired \\u2248 0.4\\u20130.7pp). | BAdam is **at least as good** if (i) fails but (ii) holds. |\\n| (iii) **Pareto (accuracy vs peak memory)** | Report (accuracy, peak GPU memory) per cell. BAdam is **Pareto-better** than LoRA on a benchmark if its accuracy lower 95 % CI bound \\u2265 LoRA's *and* its peak memory is strictly lower; otherwise we report its Pareto position. | Memory-efficient BAdam claim honored. |\\n\\nThe BAdam paper's headline thesis makes framing (i) the most aggressive hypothesis on the harder MATH benchmark and framing (iii) the headline claim on memory. Framings (i)/(ii) are reported on primary benchmarks; framing (iii) is reported on all four.\\n\\n**Operationalized hypotheses per benchmark** (formally pre-registered as the alternative for the paired test):\\n\\n* H_a (GSM8K, BAdam-Full vs LoRA-r32): \\u0394 > 0 with corrected p < 0.05.\\n* H_a (MATH, BAdam-Full vs LoRA-r32): \\u0394 > 0 with corrected p < 0.05.\\n* H_a (GSM8K, BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 vs LoRA-r32 [fairness mirror]): \\u0394 > 0 with corrected p < 0.05 (per (i)); non-inferiority per (ii) is the secondary claim.\\n* H_a (SVAMP; AQuA-RAT): same three framings, reported but framed as secondary (no primary claim of superiority is made).\\n\\n**Primary cells.** Two cells carry the headline claims:\\n\\n* **Cell P (primary):** LoRA(r=32, \\u03b1=64, all-linear) vs **BAdam-Full** (the BAdam paper's headline memory-efficient *full*-parameter SFT). This is the \\\"spirit of the question\\\" \\u2014 same compute envelope, different parameterization.\\n* **Cell F (fairness-mirror, secondary cell reported alongside P):** LoRA(r=32, \\u03b1=64, all-linear) vs **BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=32)** \\u2014 trainable scalar parameter count matched within 1\\u00d7 (414.2M vs 414.2M); differs from LoRA only in (a) the parameter update is restricted per active block and (b) optimizer state cycling. This isolates \\\"is block-wise update dynamics the lever?\\\" from \\\"does full-parameter training matter?\\\".\\n\\nBoth cells are reported for all three framings.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (B) Materials\\n\\n### B.1 Model\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Subject model | `meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-70B` (the **base / pretrained / non-Instruct** checkpoint) |\\n| Precision | **bf16** for all frozen weights and forward/backward; **fp32 adapters** for LoRA / BAdam-Block-LoRA; **fp32 master + bf16 param** for full-param blocks in BAdam-Full |\\n| Training regime | Standard causal-LM next-token prediction with teacher-forcing on the MathInstruct response (prompt tokens masked in loss) |\\n| Tokenizer | the model's own BPE (tiktoken-backed in HF `transformers`); pad token = the dedicated `<|reserved_special_token_\\u2026|>` if none set; **no chat-template tokens** are added at train or eval because the base model is not chat-tuned |\\n| License / gating access | Llama-3 Community License; HF gated model \\u2014 assumed **access granted** for design-only purposes; verification required before execution |\\n\\n### B.2 Dataset\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Training data | **TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct** (Yue et al., 2023) \\u2014 combined instruction-response set from 13 math-reasoning sources, ~262K total |\\n| HF path | `TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct` |\\n| License | Per source (Apache-2.0 / CC-BY-SA / research-only mix). Documented per HF card; if any source is research-only it is preserved but flagged in results. |\\n| Hash pinning | Pin `revision` + `sha` of the dataset before training |\\n| Pre-processing | (a) deduplicate near-duplicates (`minhash` 32-bands, Jaccard \\u2265 0.85 collapses); (b) drop items whose response is empty or non-mathematical per a regex preprocess; (c) standardize to `{instruction, output}` two-field schema |\\n| **Primary condition** | **Stratified 80K subsample** preserving the 13-source proportions; trained for **2 epochs** (\\u2248 160K example passes) |\\n| **Dev split** | Held-out 2K examples from MathInstruct train, sampled *before* any contamination check on benchmarks; used for early-stop and dev-loss tracking only (no test items) |\\n| **Scale-robustness condition** | Full 262K, 1 epoch; reported as a secondary arm to confirm (i) does not reverse at scale |\\n\\n### B.3 Hardware assumption (resolves U7)\\n\\n| Item | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Assumed envelope | **2\\u20134\\u00d7 H100 or H200 80 GB** with NVLink/NVSwitch |\\n| Frameworks | DeepSpeed **ZeRO-3** + **gradient checkpointing** + **bf16 mixed precision** |\\n| BAdam-Full cell | Requires **upper bound** (3\\u20134\\u00d7 H100) with **8-bit AdamW** (`bitsandbytes.optim.AdamW8bit`) for the active block's optimizer state |\\n| Fallback | Reduce seeds to **3** if budget is constrained; document the seed-number change before pre-registration is broken |\\n\\n### B.4 Unknown-but-used assets (no execution implied)\\n\\nWe **only describe behaviorally**; we do not assume their internal correctness:\\n* The `peft` LoRA implementation (Hu et al., 2021 re-impl) \\u2713 in `peft.LoraConfig`.\\n* The BAdam implementation, referenced as Luo et al. 2024 (paper): block-wise AdamW with CPU-offload/rotation of inactive-block optimizer state. We require that the implementation expose: (i) block count `B`, (ii) `warmup_steps_before_rotation`, (iii) the rotation schedule, (iv) per-block activation bf16. Cross-checked once via a 1B smoke test before the 70B run; if behavior deviates from paper, design is held and a new pre-registration is written.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (C) Methods\\n\\n### C.1 Baseline arm \\u2014 LoRA (PEFT)\\n\\n```\\nLoRA config         : r \\u2208 {16, 32, 64} ; \\u03b1 = 2r ; dropout = 0.05\\nTrainable targets   : all linear projections\\n                       {q_proj, k_proj, v_proj, o_proj,\\n                        gate_proj, up_proj, down_proj}\\n                       in every Transformer block (80 layers)\\nFrozen              : embed_tokens, LM head, RMSNorm, all base linear weights\\nOptimizer           : AdamW(\\u03b2=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0) \\u2014 applies only to adapters\\nLR schedule         : linear warmup 3% of total \\u2192 cosine to 0\\nPeak LR search      : {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}; 1 seed per cell; chosen = lowest dev loss;\\n                       threshold:   |\\u0394dev_loss| < 2% \\u2192 pick smaller LR;\\n                                   otherwise pick the LR with min dev loss.\\nFrozen headline LR : chosen LR frozen for the 5-seed headline run.\\nHeadline            : r=32, \\u03b1=64 (414.2 M trainable scalar params \\u2014 see computation below)\\nSensitivities       : r=16 (207.1 M), r=64 (828.4 M)\\n```\\n\\n**Trainable-parameter count derivation (used for budget matching):** with Llama-3-70B hidden=8192, kv_heads=8 (head_dim=128), intermediate=28672, 80 layers, the all-linear target module total is \\u2248 68.45 B trainable target params (Q+O = 8192\\u00b2 \\u00d7 2 per layer = 134.2 M; K+V = 8192 \\u00d7 1024 \\u00d7 2 per layer = 16.8 M; MLP = 3 \\u00d7 8192 \\u00d7 28672 per layer \\u2248 704.6 M; total per layer \\u2248 855.6 M; \\u00d780 \\u2248 68.45 B). LoRA adds `r \\u00d7 (in+out)` per module per layer; summing over all seven modules per layer \\u00d7 80 layers yields **414.2 M at r=32**, 207.1 M at r=16, 828.4 M at r=64.\\n\\n### C.2 Primary arm \\u2014 BAdam-Full (full-parameter block-wise Adam)\\n\\n```\\nBlock partition    : 10 blocks; 8 contiguous Transformer layers per block\\nRotation schedule  : cyclic, block index advances by 1 every optimizer step\\nWarmup             : step-wise warm-up of LR for 3% of total optimizer steps;\\n                       then 1 epoch (sequential) of \\\"warm-up the first block to convergence\\\" before rotation begins (matches paper's 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup)\\nOptimizer          : AdamW(\\u03b2=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0); 8-bit AdamW (bitsandbytes)\\n                     for the *active* block's optimizer state only;\\n                     inactive-block optimizer states are CPU-offloaded\\nParam representation: per-parameter master copy in fp32 + bf16 weight buffer\\nTrainable          : all 70B parameters (excluding embed_tokens and LM head which are frozen throughout)\\nLR schedule        : linear warmup 3% of total \\u2192 cosine to 0\\nPeak LR search     : {1e-5, 2e-5}    (tightened vs LoRA: per paper intuition\\n                                       that full-parameter SFT needs smaller LR)\\nHeadline           : LR chosen by dev-loss; frozen for 5-seed headline run\\n```\\n\\n### C.3 Fairness-mirror arm \\u2014 BAdam-Block-LoRA\\n\\n```\\nBaseline block part.: 10 contiguous layer blocks, 8 layers/block\\nRotation + warmup   : identical to BAdam-Full\\nTrainable targets   : the LoRA adapters inside the active block only; at every\\n                       step, only that block's adapters receive gradients; the\\n                       inactive blocks' adapters are forward-passed and\\n                       skipped from optimizer state\\nOptimizer           : AdamW(\\u03b2=(0.9, 0.999), wd=0); fp32 (small)\\nLoRA config         : identical r, \\u03b1, dropout to the *paired* LoRA cell\\nParam-budget match  : LoRA(r=32) all-linear: 414.2 M trainable;\\n                       BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=32): 414.2 M trainable (exact match);\\n                       BAdam-Block-LoRA(r=16, r=64) as sensitivities\\n                       (\\u2264 2\\u00d7 budget rule \\u21d2 r=32 is the headlined choice)\\n```\\n\\n### C.4 Alignment variables (locked)\\n\\n| Aligned variable | How it is matched | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Total tokens seen | identical (same packed-sequence bucketing: pack to 4096 tokens, first-fit; 80K examples \\u2248 10K packed sequences; 2 epochs \\u2248 20K seqs) | drives compute |\\n| Optimizer steps | identical | batch size fixed at 2 micro \\u00d7 4 grad-accum = 8 effective batch per \\\"packed sequence\\\" = effective token batch = 32 768 |\\n| Effective batch | identical | 32 768 tokens/step; 80K condition \\u2192 ~625 steps/epoch \\u00d7 2 epochs \\u2248 1 250 steps |\\n| Wall-clock-per-token | identical (same ZeRO-3, same activation checkpointing, same bf16 precision); BAdam uses 8-bit Adam where LoRA uses fp32 | reported measured wall-clock per cell as Pareto covariate |\\n| Data order | same per-seed seed-init RNG; same shuffled indices fed to both paired cells | ensures *paired* random effects |\\n| Tokenization | identical tokenizer call, identical prompt template | no leakage from prompt variance |\\n| Init of trainable params | stable seed init, frozen base seed | matched |\\n| Eval template / extractor / decoding | identical across cells | matched |\\n| Peak GPU memory | **not** matched \\u2014 measured as the Pareto covariate (this is exactly what (iii) reports) |  |\\n\\n**What is not matched (intentionally).**\\n\\n* Peak LR is allowed to differ *between methods* (each method's LR is tuned to its own dev loss within {1e-5, 2e-5, [5e-5 only for LoRA]}); once chosen, LR is frozen across methods for headline comparisons.\\n* Trainable parameter count is *intentionally* imbalanced between Cell P's two arms (414M vs 70B) because that is the question; it is *intentionally* balanced within Cell F (414M vs 414M).\\n\\n### C.5 Training schedule (locked)\\n\\n```\\nTraining objective  : causal LM NLL on response tokens (prompt masked via labels=-100)\\nSequence handling   : pack to 4096-token sequences; positional + causal masks built\\n                       correctly with attention island isolation across documents (flash-attn var-len)\\nDropout             : adapter dropout 0.05 (LoRA, BAdam-Block-LoRA);\\n                       0 (full-param blocks have no per-layer dropout in BAdam-Full \\u2014\\n                          paper convention)\\nGradient clipping   : global L2-norm 1.0\\nMixed precision     : bf16 forward + bf16 backward; fp32 master weights where used;\\n                       8-bit AdamW for active block in BAdam-Full\\nCheckpointing       : activation checkpointing on every Transformer block\\nCheckpoint cadence  : every 250 steps; keep best-3 by dev loss; final = best-by-dev\\nEarly-stop          : patience 3 evals (each eval = 1/4 epoch step on dev slice)\\nTotal tokens        : 80K condition \\u00d7 ~410 avg tokens \\u00d7 2 epochs \\u2248 65.6 M tokens\\n                       (exact token count recorded at run time and reused for full-262K scale arm)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (D) Evaluation\\n\\n### D.1 Benchmark panel\\n\\n| Tier | Benchmark | Size | Task | Primary metric |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| **Primary** | GSM8K (Cobbe et al., 2021) | 1 319 test | grade-school word-problems, free-form | exact-match @ greedy |\\n| **Primary** | MATH (Hendrycks et al., 2021) | 5 000 test | competition math, free-form with `\\\\boxed{}` | exact-match via MATH-grader equivalence (`minervamath` or `math_verify`) |\\n| Secondary | SVAMP (Patel et al., 2021) | 300 test | arithmetic word-problem variations | exact-match @ greedy |\\n| Secondary | AQuA-RAT (Ling et al., 2017) | 254 test | algebra, multiple-choice A\\u2013E | option-accuracy (single letter) |\\n\\nAQuA-RAT is **dropped from primary analyses if its contamination rate exceeds 2 %** (resolves U6). It remains in secondary. SVAMP is similarly audited.\\n\\n### D.2 Prompting\\n\\n* **Single canonical template (frozen).** For all benchmarks, all seeds, all cells:\\n\\n  ```\\n  Q: <question>\\n  A:\\n  ```\\n\\n* The base model is **not chat-tuned**, so no chat-template tokens are inserted. Sensitivity at eval (a separate secondary table) repeats evaluation under the MathInstruct SFT format:\\n\\n  ```\\n  <|begin_of_text|>Instruction: <question>\\n  Response:\\n  ```\\n\\n* **Zero-shot.** No in-context examples. Few-shot = 0 (this is required by the research question).\\n\\n### D.3 Decoding\\n\\n* Greedy decoding, `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0.0`.\\n* `max_new_tokens = 512` for MATH; `256` for GSM8K, SVAMP, AQuA.\\n* Stop on EOS or `\\\\n\\\\nQ:` (whichever comes first).\\n* No length penalty; no repetition penalty.\\n\\n### D.4 Answer extraction (identical across cells)\\n\\nA two-stage deterministic extractor:\\n\\n1. **`\\\\boxed{\\u2026}` regex** \\u2192 first match used (MATH primary path). Multi-box collapse by chosen-order.\\n2. **`The answer is X`** regex \\u2192 fallback (covers a subset of GSM8K / SVAMP that includes verbal answer phrases).\\n3. **Last-number-in-string** fallback (GSM8K / SVAMP).\\n4. **Letter regex `\\\\(?[A-E]\\\\)?`** for AQuA: pick the last matched option.\\n\\nThe same extractor is run on both LoRA and BAdam outputs; outputs are normalized via Unicode normalization and `\\\\frac{a}{b}` \\u2192 `a/b` standardization before exact-match scoring (MATH only).\\n\\n### D.5 Contamination gate (resolves U6)\\n\\n1. **Construction.** For each candidate `{test item, prompt + expected answer}` and each MathInstruct *train* example, compute:\\n    * 25-gram character overlap (with whitespace tokens) on the prompt string;\\n    * full-string match `prompt == train.string`;\\n    * answer-token Jaccard \\u2265 0.9 on answer-bearing sub-strings.\\n2. **Drop rule.** Drop any test item **before scoring** if any one of (a) \\u226525-gram prompt overlap, (b) full-string match on prompt, (c) answer Jaccard \\u2265 0.9.\\n3. **Reporting.** Per benchmark: contamination rate = (dropped) / (original test size) reported with the headline numbers.\\n4. **Hard gate.** AQuA-RAT \\u2192 if contamination rate > 2 %, AQuA is moved from secondary to *excluded* (reported only as a footnote). Same rule for SVAMP.\\n5. **GSM8K / MATH test sets** are held-out by construction in their original releases; expected contamination \\u2248 0.\\n\\n### D.6 What gets logged\\n\\nPer run, per benchmark: per-item prompt, response, extracted answer, gold answer, exact-match verdict, tokens-decoded, seconds. Saved as a CSV/JSONL log; aggregated into per-seed accuracy and per-cell accuracy tables.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (E) Statistical analysis plan\\n\\n### E.1 Sample size and pairing\\n\\n* `n_seeds = 5` per cell (default; 3 if budget-constrained, with pre-registration update).\\n* Each seed `s` produces paired observations `(LoRA_s, BAdam_s)` trained on the identical seed-init token stream. Differences are computed cell-wise.\\n\\n### E.2 Test statistic\\n\\nFor each benchmark B and Cell P or F:\\n\\n1. Compute \\u0394_s(B) = accuracy_s(BAdam, B) \\u2212 accuracy_s(LoRA, B) for s = 1..5.\\n2. **Normality check** on \\u0394_s(B) via Shapiro\\u2013Wilk. If p \\u2265 0.05 \\u2192 use **paired t-test** (two-sided for non-inferiority, one-sided for superiority). If p < 0.05 \\u2192 fall back to **Wilcoxon signed-rank** (two-sided for non-inferiority, one-sided for superiority).\\n3. **Effect size:** **Cohen's d_z** = mean(\\u0394_s(B)) / SD(\\u0394_s(B)).\\n4. **Confidence interval:** **BCa bootstrap** on \\u0394_s(B) with B = 10 000 resamples, 95 % CI.\\n5. **Multiple-comparisons correction:** within each cell, the four benchmarks are corrected by **Holm\\u2013Bonferroni** at \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n\\n### E.3 Acceptance verdicts (all three framings)\\n\\n* **(i) Superiority.** BAdam wins on a benchmark if \\u0394\\u0302 > 0 **and** corrected p < 0.05 (one-sided). Headline answer (i) = at least one primary benchmark wins.\\n* **(ii) Non-inferiority** (margin m = 1 pp = 0.01). BAdam is non-inferior on B if |\\u0394\\u0302 \\u2212 0| \\u2264 m and the **two-sided** corrected test p > 0.05 (i.e., we cannot reject the null that |\\u0394| > m). Headline (ii) = both primary benchmarks meet it.\\n* **(iii) Pareto.** Plot accuracy (95 % CI) vs peak GPU memory per cell, per primary benchmark. BAdam is Pareto-better on B iff its lower-CI bound \\u2265 LoRA's accuracy **and** its peak memory is strictly lower; otherwise report Pareto position.\\n\\n### E.4 Pre-registration\\n\\nBefore any training: write the pre-registration document listing\\n* all locked hyperparameters (LR, schedule, batch, BCa iterations, Holm family size),\\n* the exact extractor / decoder / prompt template,\\n* the contamination rule and per-benchmark drop counts,\\n* the seed list,\\n* the three pre-registered framings and their decision thresholds.\\n\\nCommit the pre-registration (hash-pinned) before unlocking training. Any later change re-opens pre-registration and is reported with explicit `deviation` tags.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (F) Design matrix table\\n\\n| ID | Cell | Method | r | Other LoRA params | BAdam block config | Training data | Epochs | n_seeds | LR (peak) | Eval panel |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| P-LoRA-hd | Cell P | LoRA all-linear (headline) | **32** | \\u03b1=64, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | tuned \\u2208 {1e-5, 2e-5, 5e-5}, then frozen | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\\n| P-BAdam-Full | Cell P | BAdam-Full | n/a | n/a | 10 blocks, cyclic, 8 layers/block, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | tuned \\u2208 {1e-5, 2e-5}, then frozen | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\\n| F-LoRA-hd | Cell F | LoRA all-linear (headline) | **32** | \\u03b1=64, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | shared LR (same cell P-LoRA-hd lock) | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\\n| F-BAdam-Block-LoRA-hd | Cell F | BAdam-Block-LoRA | **32** | \\u03b1=64, dropout=0.05 | 10 blocks, cyclic, 8 layers/block, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup | 80K subsample | 2 | **5** | shared LR | GSM8K, MATH, SVAMP, AQuA |\\n| Sens-LoRA-r16 | sensitivity | LoRA all-linear | 16 | \\u03b1=32, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\\n| Sens-LoRA-r64 | sensitivity | LoRA all-linear | 64 | \\u03b1=128, dropout=0.05 | n/a | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\\n| Sens-BBLoRA-r16 | sensitivity | BAdam-Block-LoRA | 16 | \\u03b1=32, dropout=0.05 | same as F-block | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\\n| Sens-BBLoRA-r64 | sensitivity | BAdam-Block-LoRA | 64 | \\u03b1=128, dropout=0.05 | same as F-block | 80K subsample | 2 | 3 | tuned | GSM8K, MATH |\\n| LR-sweep * | hyper only | LoRA-r32 + BAdam-Full + BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 | 32 | \\u03b1=64 | n/a / 10 blocks | 80K subsample | 1 LR-sweep only | 1 each | full LR set above | dev only |\\n| Scale-LoRA | robustness | LoRA-r32 | 32 | \\u03b1=64 | n/a | full 262K | 1 | 3 | frozen from headline | GSM8K, MATH |\\n| Scale-BAdam-Full | robustness | BAdam-Full | n/a | n/a | same | full 262K | 1 | 3 | frozen from headline | GSM8K, MATH |\\n\\nNotes:\\n\\\\* Each LR-sweep cell only inspects dev-loss and does not enter headline inference. It costs one seed per {method \\u00d7 LR} combination but ties to the headline seeds for the chosen LR combination.\\n\\nCell counts:\\n* Headline cells: 4 (P-LoRA-hd, P-BAdam-Full, F-LoRA-hd, F-BAdam-Block-LoRA-hd) \\u00d7 5 seeds \\u00d7 4 benchmarks = **80 (method, seed, benchmark) headline observations**.\\n* Sensitivity cells: 4 \\u00d7 3 seeds \\u00d7 2 benchmarks = **24 sensitivity observations**.\\n* Robustness cells: 2 \\u00d7 3 seeds \\u00d7 2 benchmarks = **12 robustness observations**.\\n* Total headline + secondary: **116 (method, seed, benchmark) cells**, none of them executed in this design-only task.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (G) Feasibility & cost (envelope estimates; design-only)\\n\\nThe numbers below are upfront envelope estimates to inform whether the design is realistic; they are not outcomes of the experiment.\\n\\n### G.1 Memory estimates per cell (peak GPU memory, single training step, seq=4096, micro-batch=2)\\n\\n| Item | LoRA-r32 (3-4\\u00d7 H100) | BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 (3-4\\u00d7 H100) | BAdam-Full (3-4\\u00d7 H100 80GB + 8-bit Adam) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Frozen base weights (bf16) | 70B \\u00d7 2B = 140 GB shard via ZeRO-3; per-GPU: 35\\u201347 GB | same | same |\\n| Active block fp32 master weights (1 block, fp32) | n/a | 8 layers \\u00d7 855.6M \\u00d7 4B \\u2248 27.4 GB on one device | same on one device |\\n| Active block bf16 weight buffer (1 block) | n/a | 8 \\u00d7 855.6M \\u00d7 2B \\u2248 13.7 GB (one device) | same (one device) |\\n| Adapters (LoRA / BAdam-Block-LoRA, fp32) | 414.2M \\u00d7 4B \\u2248 1.66 GB | 1.66 GB (one device) | n/a |\\n| Active-block optimizer state (8-bit) | n/a | n/a (fp32 AdamW since small) | 8 \\u00d7 855.6M \\u00d7 2 \\u00d7 1B \\u2248 13.7 GB on one device |\\n| Optimizer state (fp32 m+v) for adapters | 414.2M \\u00d7 8B \\u2248 3.31 GB | 3.31 GB on one device | n/a |\\n| Active-block gradients (bf16, ZeRO-3 shard) | n/a | small (one block's adapter grads only) | 8 \\u00d7 855.6M \\u00d7 2B \\u00d7 (1/4 shard) \\u2248 6.85 GB per device |\\n| Adapter gradients (fp32) | 1.66 GB | 1.66 GB | n/a |\\n| Activations (with grad ckpt, seq=4096, mb=2) | 5\\u201310 GB per GPU | 5\\u201310 GB | 10\\u201320 GB per GPU |\\n| Misc / framework overhead | 2 GB | 2 GB | 4 GB |\\n| **Peak per-GPU estimate** | **~50\\u201360 GB** (2\\u00d7 H100 feasible; 3\\u20134\\u00d7 H100 comfortable) | **~50\\u201360 GB** (2\\u00d7 H100 feasible; 3\\u20134\\u00d7 H100 comfortable) | **~70\\u201395 GB** at the device hosting the active block; **3\\u20134\\u00d7 H100 80GB required** |\\n\\n### G.2 Wall-clock estimates (design-only envelope)\\n\\nAssumptions: 4\\u00d7H100 80GB, bf16, ZeRO-3, grad ckpt, internal-throughput \\u2248 1.5\\u00d7 A100 (rule of thumb).\\n\\n| Step | LoRA-r32 / BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32 per step | BAdam-Full per step |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| fwd+bwd+optimizer | ~3\\u20135 s/step (with ZeRO-3 all-gather) | ~6\\u201310 s/step (full-param fwd+bwd dominates) |\\n| Total steps (headline) | \\u2248 1 250 step | \\u2248 1 250 step |\\n| **One seed train (headline)** | ~1.5\\u20132 hours | ~3\\u20134 hours |\\n| **Five-seed train (headline)** | ~7.5\\u201310 hours | ~15\\u201320 hours |\\n| Eval (4 benchmarks \\u00d7 5 seeds, full test sets) | ~1.5\\u20133 hours | ~1.5\\u20133 hours |\\n| Per-cell total (headline, 5 seeds + eval) | ~9\\u201313 hours | ~17\\u201323 hours |\\n\\nAggregate envelope (design-only):\\n* Cell P headline (LoRA-r32): **~10 H100-hours**.\\n* Cell P headline (BAdam-Full): **~20 H100-hours**.\\n* Cell F headline (BAdam-Block-LoRA-r32): **~10 H100-hours**.\\n* All sensibility + LR sweep + 80K condition: **~+15 H100-hours**.\\n* Full-262K robustness \\u00d7 2 methods \\u00d7 3 seeds: **~+30 H100-hours**.\\n* **Total envelope:** **~80\\u201390 H100-hours**, fitting in ~24 h on a 4\\u00d7H100 node.\\n\\n### G.3 Risk mitigations (design-only)\\n\\n1. **Reduce to 3 seeds if 5-seed envelope is breached.** Document the deviation explicitly in the pre-registration deviation log; verify power by recomputing BCa CI half-width at the realized \\u03c3_paired.\\n2. **Smoke-test on 1B before 70B.** Run 1 hour on a 1B-class base (Llama-3.2-1B or LLaMA-2-7B) to validate the BAdam implementation behaves as the paper describes (block cycle, 1-epoch pre-rotation, off-loader turnover). Pre-registration break is not required for this 1B smoke test (its outcomes are not in the headline analysis).\\n3. **Checkpoint frequently.** Every 250 steps, keep best-3 by dev loss.\\n4. **Implement a step-time and memory logger** to validate Pareto covariate (framing iii) is comparable across cells.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (H) Threats to validity\\n\\n* **Contamination.** GSM8K/MATH test sets are held out, but MathInstruct was *built from various training sets*; the audit per \\u00a7D.5 with a 2 % hard gate is the primary mitigation. AQuA and SVAMP are uplift-prone; their possible exclusion is pre-registered.\\n* **Prompt sensitivity.** A single canonical template (no chat tokens) is used to remove prompt variance. A sensitivity secondary table repeats evaluation under the MathInstruct SFT format; if results disagree, the template used to train (and the base model's non-instruction-tuned nature) is the most plausible cause and is reported as such.\\n* **Single-model, single-tokenizer.** Llama-3-70B base is one model; generalization to, e.g., Mistral, Qwen, or Llama-3.1-Instruct is not tested in this design. We do not claim cross-model generalization.\\n* **MathInstruct source overlap with SVAMP / AQuA.** Even after the contamination drop rule, residual topical / structural overlap can favor methods that better memorize the train distribution. Mitigation: we report SVAMP/AQuA as secondary only, and we report SVAMP/AQuA exclusion rate on the headline table.\\n* **Seed variance at 70B PEFT scale.** With ~10 8 -bit trainable parameters (LoRA-r16) to ~10 9 trainable parameters (LoRA-r32), gradient noise on a 41M-token training budget is the dominant source of variance. 5-seed paired design is the primary mitigation; fallback at 3 seeds is documented.\\n* **Method-implementation deviation, not method itself.** BAdam-Full's per-step behavior is sensitive to block-count, rotation policy, and offload scheduling. We adopt the paper's settings (U3) but acknowledge the implementation we use is **not the paper's reference implementation quirk-free**. Smoke test on a 1B model mitigates the most likely failure modes.\\n* **LR-asymmetry.** Cell P (BAdam-Full) and Cell P (LoRA) use method-specific peak LRs tuned on dev. This is the most defensible fairness convention in PEFT literature, but is an explicit axis on which cells differ; we report the chosen LRs in the headline.\\n* **Pareto measurement.** Peak memory is what is reported; throughput (tokens/sec) is reported separately. The two Pareto axes answer slightly different sub-claims of the BAdam paper, and we report them as such; we do **not** recompute accuracy under any \\\"matched memory\\\" capping.\\n* **Skill vs. memorization.** MATH's free-form answer extraction honors the boxed-expression convention, but a model that emits verbose prose can fail extraction the same way; the extractor is identical across cells, so the differential effect cancels but absolute numbers may be lower than leader-board numbers from differently-prompted systems.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (I) Reporting plan\\n\\n**Headline table A.1 \\u2014 Accuracies per benchmark (mean \\u00b1 std across seeds; primary condition).**\\n\\n| Method | GSM8K | MATH | SVAMP | AQuA-RAT (or excluded) | Peak GPU mem |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| LoRA-r32 (headline) | mean \\u00b1 std | \\u2026 | \\u2026 | \\u2026 | X GB |\\n| BAdam-Full | \\u2026 | \\u2026 | \\u2026 | \\u2026 | Y GB |\\n| (\\u0394, 95% BCa CI, p_holm) | per col | per col | per col | per col | n/a |\\n\\n**Headline table A.2 \\u2014 Paired-difference verdicts.**\\n\\n| Benchmark | \\u0394\\u0302 (BAdam \\u2212 LoRA) | BCa 95 % CI | Cohen's d_z | corrected p | Verdict (i) sup | Verdict (ii) NI | Verdict (iii) Pareto |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n\\nCell P and Cell F each have one row per benchmark.\\n\\n**Figure plan.**\\n* **Fig. 1** \\u2014 per-benchmark paired-difference forest plot (Cell P, primary condition) with 95 % BCa CIs and the 1pp non-inferiority band.\\n* **Fig. 2** \\u2014 Pareto plot: accuracy on MATH vs peak GPU memory, one marker per (cell, seed), color-coded by method.\\n* **Fig. 3** \\u2014 training curves (dev loss and benchmark accuracy on a held-out 500-example MATH slice) over the 1 250-step run, averaged across seeds; shaded = 95 % BCa band.\\n\\n**Sensitivity table A.3.**\\n* Sensitivity (`r = 16`, `r = 32`, `r = 64`) of LoRA and BAdam-Block-LoRA on the two primary benchmarks; same statistics.\\n\\n**Robustness table A.4.**\\n* Full-262K condition, LoRA-r32 vs BAdam-Full on GSM8K and MATH (3 seeds); only used to cross-check (i)/(ii) does not flip sign at scale.\\n\\n**Side table A.5** \\u2014 per-benchmark contamination rate and excluded item count.\\n\\n**Verdict mapping to the research question** is reported as a single sentence per framing:\\n\\n* (i) \\\"BAdam **does better than** LoRA on the math benchmark {GSM8K | MATH | neither}\\\" iff the one-sided corrected test on \\u2265 1 primary benchmark passes.\\n* (ii) \\\"BAdam is **at least as good as** LoRA on the math panel\\\" iff both primary benchmarks pass the 1pp NI check.\\n* (iii) \\\"BAdam is **a Pareto-improved fit** for this fine-tune\\\" iff accuracy is non-inferior AND peak memory is strictly lower on at least one primary benchmark.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (J) References\\n\\n1. Cobbe, K., Kosaraju, V., Bavarian, M., Chen, M., Jun, H., Kaiser, L., Plappert, M., Tworek, J., Hilton, J., Nakano, R., Hesse, C., & Schulman, J. (2021). *Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems.* arXiv:2110.14168.\\n2. Hendrycks, D., Burns, C., Kadavath, S., Arora, A., Basart, S., Tang, E., Song, D., & Steinhardt, J. (2021). *Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset.* NeurIPS Datasets and Benchmarks.\\n3. Hu, E. J., Shen, Y., Wallis, P., Allen-Zhu, Z., Li, Y., Wang, S., Wang, L., & Chen, W. (2021). *LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.* ICLR.\\n4. Ling, W., Yogatama, D., Dyer, C., & Blunsom, P. (2017). *Program Induction by Rationale Generation: Learning to Solve and Explain Algebraic Word Problems.* ACL.\\n5. Luo, Q., Yu, X., & others. (2024). *BAdam: A Memory-Efficient Full Parameter Optimization of Large Language Models.* arXiv.\\n6. Meta AI. (2024). *The Llama 3 Herd of Models.* (referenced for tokenizer, config, model card).\\n7. Patel, A., Bhattamishra, S., & Goyal, N. (2021). *Are NLP Models really able to Solve Simple Math Word Problems?* NAACL.\\n8. Yue, X., Qu, X., Zhang, G., Fu, Y., Huang, S., Liu, J., Chen, W., Zeng, J., Wang, L., WANG, Y., Tian, B., Cao, M., Zhang, D., Cao, Y., Yan, J., Liu, T., Ji, S., Chen, Y., & Liu, P. (2023). *MAmmoTH: Building Math Reasoning Models with Hybrid Instruction Data.* (paper releasing MathInstruct as the associated dataset.) arXiv:2309.05653.\\n9. Holm, S. (1979). *A simple sequentially rejective multiple test procedure.* Scandinavian Journal of Statistics.\\n10. Efron, B. (1987). *Better bootstrap confidence intervals.* JASA. (BCa bootstrap reference.)\\n11. `peft` library documentation \\u2014 HuggingFace, LoRA implementation.\\n12. `bitsandbytes` 8-bit optimizer documentation \\u2014 for AdamW8bit used in BAdam-Full's active block.\\n13. DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 documentation \\u2014 Microsoft.\\n14. *MathInstruct* dataset card \\u2014 `TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct` on HuggingFace.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Closing note (researcher-facing)\\n\\nThis document is design-only. **No experiment was executed and no outcome is reported or implied.** Estimated wall-clock, memory, and parameter counts in (G) are upfront feasibility envelopes to inform whether the design fits the hardware assumption (B.3 / U7); they will be re-validated by the smoke test (G.3 item 2) before any headline run. The pre-registration content (E.4) is the only authoritative document for headline inference; the table in (F) lists every (method, config, seed, benchmark) cell that would exist if the experiment were run.\\n\\nAll resolved open questions are written as **fixed design choices** above; what remains assumption-shaped is enumerated in the `assumptions[]` of this handoff for completeness \\u2014 these are not open questions for the design but are conditions on its scope.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Environment evidence: /workspace contains only /workspace/task.json; no track.md file on disk (the documents.track.md field is metadata stating \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"); bubblewrap runtime with disabled network, GPU disabled; Python 3.12.3 with 24 cores, ~125 GiB RAM, ~775 GiB free disk; no ML libraries installed (torch/transformers/peft/trl/vllm absent); HF auth tokens absent; git/pip/curl/wget available. The research question and the design-only instruction are confirmed in /workspace/task.json; no executed experiment was performed. Budget math (414.2 M LoRA-r32 trainable params, 68.45 B target-module total) was computed live in this session from Llama-3-70B's published config (hidden=8192, kv_heads=8, intermediate=28672, layers=80, all-linear targets) to make Cell F's exact trainable-params match internally consistent in (C.D design plan).\", \"assumptions\": [\"X1. With Llama-3-70B base (hidden=8192, kv_heads=8, intermediate=28672, layers=80, all-linear LoRA targets), LoRA-r32 yields 414.2 M trainable scalar params and the all-linear target module total is ~68.45 B; used only for budget matching in (C.3) and memory bookkeeping in (G.1).\", \"X2. HF gated access to Llama-3-70B base is assumed granted for execution purposes; not used in this design-only deliverable.\", \"X3. The exact per-step wall-clock-per-token in (G.2) is an upfront envelope estimate based on rule-of-thumb throughput for 4 x H100 80GB NVLink at 70B bf16 + ZeRO-3; not a measured outcome.\", \"X4. Reported typical 5-seed paired std for LoRA-r32 ~ 0.4-0.7pp on GSM8K/MATH comes from analogous PEFT-vs-LoRA comparisons in the BAdam paper and Hu et al. follow-ups; the 1pp non-inferiority margin in (A)/(E.3) is grounded in this range but is itself a pre-registered design choice.\", \"X5. The implementation of BAdam available to us matches Luo et al. 2024's paper-described behavior (block-wise AdamW with cyclic rotation, 1-epoch pre-rotation warmup, CPU offload for inactive-block optimizer state); any deviation is caught by the 1B smoke test (G.3 item 2) before 70B execution.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"R1 (informational, low). Llama-3-70B HF gated access verification is required before execution; design-only deliverable does not depend on this.\", \"R2 (informational, low). Exact revision/sha pin of TIGER-Lab/MathInstruct is fixed at pre-registration time, not at design time.\", \"R3 (informational, low). Per-step wall-clock and per-device peak memory to be measured at smoke-test time, replacing the envelope in (G.2) and (G.1).\", \"R4 (informational, low). Benchmarks' \\\"official answer extraction\\\" quirks: -MATH grader equivalence (minervamath or math_verify) is locked at pre-registration. If behaviour differs across the two at MATH extraction time, the design specifies minervamath as the default since it is the version the MAmmoTH paper uses for the MathInstruct panel evaluation.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-66f8c76d-eb14-4b5d-8e48-e24fc4984c77", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 25}
